<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cyprus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cyprus is a large island in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, valued for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> it corresponded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a> diplomatic letters of the Egyptian Empire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muawiya-i">Muawiya</a>, the first Arab navy of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a> raided Byzantine Cyprus.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cyprus.jpg" alt="Cyprus" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: İsmet Güney, SVG by Vzb83 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cyprus.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Cyprus is the third largest island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. It measures 240 kilometres from end to end and 100 kilometres at its widest, and lies 75 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Two mountain ranges, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troodos-mountains">Troodos Mountains</a> and the smaller Kyrenia Range, enclose the central Mesaoria plain. The highest point is Mount Olympus, at 1,952 metres, in the Troodos range. Some sources place the island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and others in Western Asia, within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Hunter-gatherers first reached Cyprus around 13,000 to 12,000 years ago, at about the time the island's pygmy hippopotamus and dwarf elephant became extinct. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> farming communities emerged by around 8500 BCE, and the village of Khirokitia dates to approximately 6800 BCE.</p><p>From around 1650 BCE copper from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troodos-mountains">Troodos Mountains</a> drove trade and the growth of urban settlements, and the island, identified in whole or part as Alashiya, was ruled by kings who corresponded with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom of Egypt</a>, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a> document. Mycenaean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> traded there from around 1400 BCE, and a major wave of Greek settlement is believed to have followed the collapse of Mycenaean Greece.</p><p>Later the island was contested by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> seized it in 333 BCE. Ptolemaic, Roman, Arab, Lusignan and Venetian rule followed, then Ottoman dominion from 1571 to 1878. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> administered Cyprus from 1878 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus-convention">Cyprus Convention</a> and annexed it formally in 1914. Independence came in 1960. Intercommunal violence in 1963 and 1964 displaced more than 25,000 Turkish Cypriots into enclaves.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>A coup by Greek Cypriot nationalists and elements of the Greek military junta on 15 July 1974 was followed by the Turkish invasion of 20 July, which captured the north and displaced over 150,000 Greek Cypriots and 50,000 Turkish Cypriots. A Turkish Cypriot state declared in 1983 is recognised only by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. The island is divided along a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> buffer zone, and two British sovereign military bases, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akrotiri-prehistoric-city">Akrotiri</a> and Dhekelia, remain in the south.</p><p>The Republic of Cyprus, a presidential republic, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> in 2004 and adopted the euro on 1 January 2008. Greek and Turkish Cypriots share many customs, such as the foods souvla and halloumi, but differ in religion: Greek Cypriots are traditionally Greek Orthodox and Turkish Cypriots Sunni Muslim.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cyprus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 307 BCE his son Demetrius expelled Cassander's governor Demetrius of Phalerum from Athens, and in 306 BCE, after Demetrius destroyed Ptolemy's fleet off Salamis in <b>Cyprus</b>, Antigonus took the title of king and conferred it on his son, the first of the Successors to do so.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the fall, 30,000 wealthy Armenians moved to Lusignan <b>Cyprus</b> and merchant families founded or joined diaspora communities in France, Italy, the Netherlands, Poland and Spain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Second World War about 150,000 Armenians from countries such as <b>Cyprus</b>, France, Greece, Iraq, Lebanon and Syria came between 1946 and 1948.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 451 BCE Cimon led 200 ships to <b>Cyprus</b> and died during the blockade of Citium, though the fleet won a double victory off Salamis, Cyprus, the last major battle against the Persians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delian-league">Delian League · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Amarna Letters, the diplomatic archive recovered from Akhenaten's capital, show an organised system of international correspondence conducted in Akkadian cuneiform, through which Egypt managed its relationships with Babylon, Mitanni, Assyria, the Hittites, and Alashiya (<b>Cyprus</b>).</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>On 20 July 1974 Turkey invaded <b>Cyprus</b> in answer to a Greek-backed Cypriot coup.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hellenic-republic">Hellenic Republic · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
